1
00:00:06,988 --> 00:00:13,271
JAMIE: Microsoft's up fifteen point five-one percent, and that's not even the headline—turns out the AI trade is splitting the market.

2
00:00:13,271 --> 00:00:30,025
ALEX: The tape closed with the S&P at seven thousand four hundred thirty-seven, up one point six-six percent. Dow up one point one-nine percent. Nasdaq took the lead with two point seven-eight percent—the Russell two thousand keeping pace at one point three-seven. Breadth is there. The VIX dropped seventeen point two-eight percent, which means volatility evaporated.

3
00:00:30,025 --> 00:00:33,316
JAMIE: So we're just... calm now? After the week we've had?

4
00:00:33,316 --> 00:00:39,598
ALEX: You've got the classic risk-on setup: equities rallying, rates climbing, gold catching a bid because nobody's actually confident in anything.

5
00:00:39,598 --> 00:00:45,582
MAYA: Gold's the only thing that's honest about this market right now. Everything else is pretending it knows what's happening.

6
00:00:45,582 --> 00:00:53,959
JAMIE: And we're watching this whole thing with Microsoft and AMD just running—fifteen point five-one percent and thirteen percent respectively. That's not a market move, that's a stampede.

7
00:00:53,959 --> 00:01:08,320
ALEX: Oracle up eight point three-four percent, Broadcom up four point seven-three. Amazon up three point nine. Meanwhile Meta's down seven point nine-five, Adobe's off five point nine, Accenture down five point seven-one. The tape's sorting itself: infrastructure and AI chips are in, consumer-facing software and consulting are out.

8
00:01:08,320 --> 00:01:16,098
JAMIE: So Meta's guidance missed—the market looked at that and said, 'Yeah, we're done.' Eight percent down on a day when basically everything else is up.

9
00:01:16,098 --> 00:01:24,774
ALEX: They also missed revenue guidance. That's the thing people aren't screaming about loud enough. Microsoft gets a beat and guidance raise, Meta gets a beat and guidance miss.

10
00:01:24,774 --> 00:01:31,057
MAYA: And here's the problem—Meta's spending is still going up. They're burning cash on AI infrastructure while the cash flow's evaporating.

11
00:01:31,057 --> 00:01:38,836
JAMIE: But Azure and Copilot at Microsoft—those are actual revenue drivers right now. Satya's not just talking about AI, he's got paying customers. That's the split.

12
00:01:38,836 --> 00:01:47,213
ALEX: The market's drawing a line: infrastructure plays that monetize AI get rewarded, consumer-facing platforms that are still figuring out how to turn AI into cash get punished.

13
00:01:47,213 --> 00:01:52,000
MAYA: Enough that people are actually counting. The streak matters because it means the narrative's shifted.

14
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:58,881
JAMIE: And that's the thing—Microsoft gets to spend on AI and everyone cheers. Meta spends on AI and everyone asks for the exit sign.

15
00:01:58,881 --> 00:02:05,463
ALEX: The UK Supreme Court just gave Palestine Action's co-founder permission to appeal the government's proscription of the group under anti-terrorism legislation.
  ↳ source: https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c87n321w9qlo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss

16
00:02:05,463 --> 00:02:12,344
JAMIE: That's not a small thing. The government already banned them, and now the court's saying, hold on, let's look at this again.

17
00:02:12,344 --> 00:02:20,421
MAYA: Apple just posted iPhone sales up in the latest quarter, and here's the thing—they did it while the entire chip industry is drowning in RAM shortages.

18
00:02:20,421 --> 00:02:25,208
JAMIE: That's the inverse of what we usually see. Shortages hurt sales. Apple's defying the script.

19
00:02:25,208 --> 00:02:29,696
ALEX: They've got the supply chain relationships and the pricing power to absorb the shortage.

20
00:02:29,696 --> 00:02:34,782
JAMIE: Meta's stock got hammered again. Down hard on a day when the rest of tech's flying.
  ↳ source: https://www.marketwatch.com/story/metas-stock-is-falling-hard-heres-why-the-company-is-in-wall-streets-doghouse-afae2f55?mod=mw_rss_topstories

21
00:02:34,782 --> 00:02:44,057
MAYA: They beat earnings. They beat revenue. But they guided down on future revenue and they're not showing a path to profitability in the areas where they're burning the most cash.

22
00:02:44,057 --> 00:02:48,245
ALEX: The market's not punishing them for missing—it's punishing them for missing while spending more.

23
00:02:48,245 --> 00:02:54,827
JAMIE: Tim Cook just said Apple's hybrid AI strategy is a competitive weapon. They're going to charge for artificial intelligence through iCloud.

24
00:02:54,827 --> 00:02:59,015
MAYA: That's the same bet that worked for them with music and cloud storage.

25
00:02:59,015 --> 00:03:07,093
ALEX: The difference is Apple controls the device. You want Siri AI? You're already in the ecosystem. They don't need to compete on price—they compete on integration.

26
00:03:07,093 --> 00:03:15,769
MAYA: Rivian just cut its spending plans and narrowed its losses for this year, which sounds like a win until you realize they're doing it because they're hemorrhaging cash.

27
00:03:15,769 --> 00:03:22,351
JAMIE: So they're pulling back on capital expenditure—that's the infrastructure spend, the factory buildout. What does that actually mean for production timelines?

28
00:03:22,351 --> 00:03:26,839
ALEX: It means they're slowing down the thing that was supposed to justify their valuation.

29
00:03:26,839 --> 00:03:35,814
MAYA: Meta's lost ground while Microsoft prints money on the same bet. The market's decided which company gets to gamble with shareholder cash and which one has to actually win.

30
00:03:35,814 --> 00:03:41,200
ALEX: One's building the infrastructure the other one needs. The other one's hoping the infrastructure eventually does something.

31
00:03:41,200 --> 00:03:45,388
JAMIE: This show is for entertainment and education only — nothing here is investment advice.
